<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The one colour that cannot live in Color.kt.

      A window background is painted by the platform before any Compose frame exists, so it has to
      be an XML resource. Without it the window inherits the platform parent's #303030, which flashes
      a lighter grey on cold start and shows behind the app in the recents snapshot.

      This MUST equal HabitsDarkColorScheme.background. ThemeTest asserts exactly that, so the two
      cannot drift apart silently.
    -->
    <color name="window_background">#FF111113</color>

    <!--
      The launcher icon's two colours. Same problem as the window background above: a resource XML
      cannot read Color.kt.

      The GROUND is an alias, not a second copy of the hex — the icon's ground IS the app's
      background, so aliasing means it cannot drift from window_background or from Compose. The
      final identity asks for #111113 and window_background already is #FF111113, so the design and
      the app agreed here without anyone changing anything.

      THE MARK IS WHITE, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* A PALETTE COLOUR.

      Until M14.1 this was the app's primary blue, with a test asserting it stayed equal to
      HabitsDarkColorScheme.primary. The final identity reverses that on purpose: a habit-coloured
      icon ties the launcher to the interface, but it also picks a favourite among four equals — blue
      is one of the four habit colours the user assigns — and it dates the identity to whatever the
      palette happens to be. Monochrome white stays true at every density and gives the themed-icon
      layer away for free.

      So the invariant inverted rather than disappeared. ThemeTest now asserts this is NOT primary,
      which is what stops a later session from "fixing" the icon back to blue and quietly undoing a
      decision the design made with its eyes open.
    -->
    <color name="ic_launcher_background">@color/window_background</color>
    <color name="ic_launcher_mark">#FFFFFFFF</color>
</resources>
